Suppose we have a type $A$, and that $A$ is a set. This means that for any two paths $x -->^p y$ and $x -->^q y$ in $A$ (equivalently, $p : x =_A y$, $q : x =_A y$), we have a path $p -->^r q$ in the next level (the type $x =_A y$). And indeed, for any $p -->^s q$, we have a path $r --> s$ in the next level ($p =_(x = y) q$), and so on. In HoTT 3.1, they say sets are like "discrete groupoids", which have only identity paths as higher morphisms. I understand this to mean that $r$ and $s$ are identity morphisms, although if this is true then we have a metatheoretic identity of $p$ and $q$, which is stronger than there just being a path between them. So maybe they mean that the path $r --> s$ is an identity? What is the right understanding of this, and how do we go from $A$ being a set (in the type-theoretic sense) to $A$ being a discrete groupoid (all higher paths are identities)?
